# Rig module and hierarchy convention

Whenever the user asks to create, build or adapt a rig, use the project's
existing module architecture. Resolve the loaded checkout, read the area
index and inspect `maya_tools/scripts/biped/autorig/arm_module.py` or the
closest existing module before implementation.

- Use `utils/create_rig.AutoRig` and `utils/basic_structure` for the build.
- Keep one asset-named main transform, such as `flower`.
- Keep `rig_GRP`, `controls_GRP`, `geo_GRP` and `deformers_GRP` below it.
- Keep `modules_GRP`, `skel_GRP` and `skeletonHierarchy_GRP` under `rig_GRP`.
- Follow the existing class pattern: `__init__` reads shared build data;
  `make` creates the module groups and calls explicit guide, chain, control,
  setup and data-export stages. Use shared curve/matrix/guide tools.
- Each module owns its `*_Module_GRP`/`*Module_GRP`, `*Skinning_GRP` and
  `*Controllers_GRP` using the existing naming convention. Controller groups
  belong to the master control hierarchy; deformation groups belong to
  `skel_GRP`; mechanics belong to the module under `modules_GRP`.
- Populate the native `_ENV` skeleton below `skeletonHierarchy_GRP` with
  correct module attachment branches using the shared hierarchy helpers.
  An empty skeleton hierarchy is not a completed rig.
- Preserve standard guides, controller templates, skin data, visibility and
  build lifecycle. Check the actual native scene after build and reopen.
- Animator-facing PV and eye aim lines stay under their controls; never move
  them below hidden module/construction groups.
- Honor the requested settings drawing. Flower uses an F-shaped
  `C_settings_CTL`, with its existing transform and channels preserved.

